  "facts": [
    "Jose Rizal Bridge, Seattle’s first permanent steel bridge, opened in 1911, originally named the 12th Avenue South Bridge, and earned a National Register of Historic Places spot in 1982 under its original name.",
    "Renamed in 1974 to honor Filipino patriot José Rizal, the bridge remains largely unknown to locals, yet its floodlights, installed during Paul Schell’s Millennium Project, illuminate the night skyline.",
    "In 2021, the bridge’s four traffic lanes were cut to two, creating protected bicycle lanes, marking a shift toward safer, multimodal urban mobility on this historic structure."
